    "summary": "Soaring Morning Dew, epic Dark Star suite, fireworks‑capped Playin’ In The Band",
    "blurb": "The June 10 1973 RFK Stadium concert launches with a soaring Morning Dew and builds into an unforgettable Dark Star → He's Gone → Wharf Rat → Truckin' marathon. Jerry’s guitar is on fire, Phil delivers monster bass lines, and the Allman guests turn the third set into a fireworks‑filled climax. A quintessential ’73 marathon for any Deadhead.",
    "review": "On June 10, 1973 at RFK Stadium the Grateful Dead delivered one of their most memorable marathon shows. The first set kicked off with an inspired take on “Morning Dew,” setting a high bar that only rose higher as the band wove through classics like “Box of Rain,” “Bird Song,” and a powerful “Playing In The Band” that closed the set with fireworks. The second set was a deep‑jam odyssey, highlighted by the seamless segue from “Dark Star” into “He's Gone,” then “Wharf Rat” and “Truckin'.” The improvisational peaks of “Eyes Of The World,” “Bird Song,” and the soaring “Here Comes Sunshine” showcase Jerry’s expressive wah‑wah leads and Phil’s monster bass work. The third set turned legendary with Allman Brothers guests, culminating in a fireworks‑capped “Playin’ In The Band” that left the stadium buzzing. Across all available recordings—especially the pristine Charlie Miller soundboard capture—the performance is crystal‑clear, balanced, and captures both the tight musicianship and the expansive, sun‑baked jam spirit of ’73. Whether you listen to the SBD or the patched matrix, this show stands out as a must‑hear snapshot of the Dead at their improvisational peak.",
